<!-- Aura-SOC - rule 100950 (level 3). Domain: misp,threat_intel,cti,
     Naming convention and load-order trap: see rules/README.md -->
<group name="misp,threat_intel,cti,">

  <!-- ==========================================================================
       CTI - MISP indicator matching (100950-100959)

       Data path: `python -m soc_agent.cti` mirrors MISP attributes and bulk
       reputation lists into a local SQLite cache; the `custom-misp` integration
       looks up every indicator carried by an alert (IP, domain, URL, file hash)
       in that cache and re-injects an enriched event here. See docs/CTI.md.

       Severity is driven by two fields, never by the indicator itself:

         misp.confidence  curated -> contextualised intelligence (CERT-FR, CIRCL,
                                     Botvrij, ThreatFox, URLhaus). A match is an
                                     incident: level 12+.
                          bulk    -> mass reputation lists (100k+ rotating IPs,
                                     no context). A match is background noise
                                     from the internet: level 10, deliberately
                                     below the incident pipeline (MIN_LEVEL=12).

         misp.direction   inbound  -> a flagged host talked to us. Common, cheap.
                          outbound -> one of OUR hosts talked to a flagged
                                      destination. That host is likely already
                                      compromised - the expensive case.
                          artifact -> a file hash on one of our machines.

       Full field set carried by every match (all under data.misp.*):

         ioc, ioc_type, field, direction, source, confidence, category,
         event_info, event_id, event_url, search_url, tags, threat_level,
         match_count, source_alert_rule_id, source_alert_description,
         agent, agent_id

       event_url points at the MISP event when the indicator is curated;
       search_url always resolves, and is the only entry point for bulk lists
       (they live in MISP's Redis cache and carry no event). Both are built
       from the PUBLIC MISP url, stored in the cache by cti.py - a link to the
       loopback address would only be clickable from the manager itself.

       Rules below are mutually exclusive by these fields (see rules/README.md:
       never rely on file order to arbitrate between siblings).
       ========================================================================== -->

  <!-- CTI lookup produced a match (any source, any direction) -->

  <rule id="100950" level="3">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="integration">custom-misp</field>
    <description>CTI - MISP indicator match: $(misp.ioc) ($(misp.ioc_type), source $(misp.source))</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
  </rule>

</group>
